# Switch Statement in Java

## Introduction

The `switch` statement is a decision-making statement used to execute one block of code from multiple available options.

It is generally used as an alternative to multiple `if-else-if` statements when checking the value of a single expression.

---

# Why Do We Need the Switch Statement?

When there are multiple possible choices for the same variable, writing many `if-else` statements can make the code lengthy and difficult to read.

The `switch` statement provides a cleaner and more organized way to handle multiple conditions.

Examples:

- Display the day of the week.
- Perform menu-driven programs.
- Execute different operations based on user choice.
- Build calculator applications.

---

# Syntax

```java
switch (expression) {

    case value1:
        // Code
        break;

    case value2:
        // Code
        break;

    case value3:
        // Code
        break;

    default:
        // Code
}
```

---

# Flow of Execution

```text
          Expression
               │
      ┌────────┼────────┐
      │        │        │
   Case 1   Case 2   Case 3
      │        │        │
      └────────┼────────┘
               │
           Default
               │
        Next Statement
```

---

# Example 1

```java
public class SwitchExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int day = 3;

        switch (day) {

            case 1:
                System.out.println("Monday");
                break;

            case 2:
                System.out.println("Tuesday");
                break;

            case 3:
                System.out.println("Wednesday");
                break;

            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid Day");

        }

    }

}
```

### Output

```text
Wednesday
```

---

# Example 2

```java
public class SwitchExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        char grade = 'A';

        switch (grade) {

            case 'A':
                System.out.println("Excellent");
                break;

            case 'B':
                System.out.println("Very Good");
                break;

            case 'C':
                System.out.println("Good");
                break;

            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid Grade");

        }

    }

}
```

### Output

```text
Excellent
```

