# The break Statement

The `break` statement is used to terminate the current case and exit the switch block.

Without `break`, Java continues executing the next cases. This is known as **fall-through**.

Example:

```java
int number = 1;

switch (number) {

    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");

    case 2:
        System.out.println("Two");

}
```

### Output

```text
One
Two
```

---

# The default Statement

The `default` block executes when none of the cases match the given expression.

Example:

```java
int number = 10;

switch (number) {

    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");
        break;

    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid Number");

}
```

### Output

```text
Invalid Number
```

